### xargs -i with attached braces
xargs -i{} echo {}
>>> SC2267 1:7

### xargs -e consumes a word then -i follows
xargs -e sed -i foo
>>- SC2267 1:14 -- GNU and BusyBox xargs take the -e end-of-file string only when attached, so -i belongs to sed
>>+ SL3002 1:17 -- shell-lsp native check; GNU xargs takes the -e end-of-file string only when attached, so it runs sed with the script foo

### upper-case -I is the replacement
xargs -I{} echo {}
>>> none

### sed -i after the command operand
xargs sed -i -e 's/http:/https:/'
>>> none

### -x then sed with -i
xargs -x sed -i 's/foo/bar/'
>>> none

### xargs -l with an attached count then -i
xargs -l1 -i echo {}
>>> SC2267 1:11

### xargs -i after a long option
